==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|mul|-}} {{taxlink|Thallophyta|division}}. Surface reading of {{suffix|en|thallus|phyte|t2=plant}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈθæləfaɪt/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|botany}} Any of very many primitive [[plant]]s that consist of a [[thallus]] (plant body not differentiated into [[root]]s, [[stem]]s and [[leaf|leaves]]), formerly collected in the obsolete taxonomic group {{taxlink|Thallophyta|group}}.
#* '''1994''', Estrelita A. Madriaga, Anthony Lee, Felicitas B. Ventura, Orlando A. Oronce (coordinator), ''Biology: Science and Technology II'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=HPCEK9ZVmEQC&pg=PA347&dq=%22thallophyte%22%7C%22thallophytes%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=eXb4Uv6NDon4lAXcq4D4CQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22thallophyte%22%7C%22thallophytes%22&f=false page 347],
#*: They<sup>[algae, fungi and mosses]</sup> lack the vascular tissues; thus, they are called '''thallophytes'''. Algae are '''thallophytes''' with chlorophyll.{{...}}Fungi are '''thallophytes''' without chlorophyll.
#* '''1994''', Pat E. Rasmussen, ''Chapter IV.5: Mercury in Vegetation of the Precambrian Shield'', Carl J. Watras, John W. Huckabee (editors), ''Mercury Pollution: Integration and Synthesis'', [http://books.google.com.au/books?id=OO0qtznwupcC&pg=PA422&dq=%22thallophyte%22%7C%22thallophytes%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=eXb4Uv6NDon4lAXcq4D4CQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22thallophyte%22%7C%22thallophytes%22&f=false page 422],
#*: In general, the usefulness of the '''thallophytes''' as Hg bioindicators was limited by their sporadic occurrence in the forest environment. Because of the importance of the '''thallophytes''' as components of the food chain, however, several samples from different groups (summarized in Table 2) were collected for comparison with values reported in the literature.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=R. G. Hermann|chapter=The Chloroplast: Part of the Integrated Genetic System of the Plant Cell|editors=Joan H. Argyroudi-Akoyunoglou; Horst Senger|title=The Chloroplast: From Molecular Biology to Biotechnology|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=VbGPXMcvWhgC&pg=PA66&dq=%22thallophyte%22%7C%22thallophytes%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=eXb4Uv6NDon4lAXcq4D4CQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22thallophyte%22%7C%22thallophytes%22&f=false|page=66
|passage=Fossile{{sic}} records along with molecular, cytological and morphological phylogenetic work favour a single origin of land plants from charophycean '''thallophytes''', and indicate that several major lineages of vascular plants had evolved more than 400 million years ago.}}

